@anon.53fe85e3a453dfbf0ab1e8743506e67c.1169 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [662 x i8] }> <{ [662 x i8] c"If this is `{false}`, the items are spaced apart with\0A[list spacing]($list.spacing). If it is `{true}`, they use normal\0A[leading]($par.leading) instead. This makes the list more compact, which\0Acan look better if the items are short.\0A\0AIn markup mode, the value of this parameter is determined based on\0Awhether items are separated with a blank line. If items directly follow\0Aeach other, this is set to `{true}`; if items are separated by a blank\0Aline, this is set to `{false}`.\0A\0A```example\0A- If a list has a lot of text, and\0A  maybe other inline content, it\0A  should not be tight anymore.\0A\0A- To make a list wide, simply insert\0A  a blank line between the items.\0A```" }>, align 1
@anon.53fe85e3a453dfbf0ab1e8743506e67c.1170 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [508 x i8] }> <{ [508 x i8] c"The marker which introduces each item.\0A\0AInstead of plain content, you can also pass an array with multiple\0Amarkers that should be used for nested lists. If the list nesting depth\0Aexceeds the number of markers, the markers are cycled. For total\0Acontrol, you may pass a function that maps the list's nesting depth\0A(starting from `{0}`) to a desired marker.\0A\0A```example\0A#set list(marker: [--])\0A- A more classic list\0A- With en-dashes\0A\0A#set list(marker: ([\E2\80\A2], [--]))\0A- Top-level\0A  - Nested\0A  - Items\0A- Items\0A```" }>, align 1
